# The layers a double count runs between, which is the evidence for an elimination. One row per
# `asrt:between` on a filed elimination: which party's filing, which layer of it, under which
# regime and which version the composer read.
#
# The subject of an elimination
#   An elimination without this is a number with no subject. `eliminations/filed.sqlc` says a
#   composer removed 90 GPU-hours from `merge-holding-composition/compute`. It cannot say what was
#   counted twice. These rows can: `compute-us` and `compute-pt`, both from `group-parent`, both
#   under the `group` regime. That is the difference between an adjustment and an argument.
#
# Why it needs a home in the database
#   Without one, the corpus's `between` rows are read from the XML and dropped on every ingest, and
#   nothing notices, because no rule reads them: a rule reads only what it needs, so a field no
#   rule reads is under no pressure to exist. Only asking "can this document be written back out"
#   finds a hole like this, which is a question the query side structurally cannot ask.
#
# No foreign key on `notation`
#   `notation` is a foreign reference and may dangle, so it carries no foreign key, exactly as
#   `composition/part_references.sqlc` does not. `asrt:Elimination` argues the point directly,
#   refusing a keyref that would restrict `between` to the fusion's own parts because "a group
#   eliminating against a member that files nothing, or against a layer folded into a different
#   composed layer, is ordinary".
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:eliminations/asrt:elimination/asrt:between, one row each.
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.quantity, b.seq,
       b.party, b.notation, b.layer, b.version, b.regime,
       b.registration_taxonomy, b.registration_value
FROM pm.elimination_between b
